into a TV series in  2009 Rhys told Gizmodo we wrote it as a  TV pilot and sold it to CBS they had a  lot of notes which we addressed and then  when they decided to not make the Pilot  We unaddressed a lot of them we went  back to what we had some were during the  rewriting process Reese and wernick  decided to turn their pilot script into  a movie screenplay instead years later  the duo got another shot at television  and filmed an episode for a spin-off as  part of Amazon's 2014 pilot program  while reviewers had kind words the  streaming service passed on turning it  into a full series  some TV to movie Evolutions come about  when a producer feels an idea is too  good to be limited to the small screen  others move their show to a future film  when a producer has connections with a  major movie studio however the 2020  gangster biopic Capone took a longer and  more complicated path it took more than  30 years two entirely different projects  and the influence of Hollywood star Tom  Hardy to get the movie made the journey  began in the 1970s with a TV pilot for a  show called Cicero the proposed series  was created by the Wild Bunch scribe  Whalen green after decades in limbo it  was finally picked up by Warner Brothers  in 2010 by which Time The Script had  evolved into a screenplay for a big  budget motion picture a year later Hardy  signed on with Harry Potter director  David Yates slated to help the project  things never fell into place however and  the project was ultimately scrapped  despite this setback party had fallen so  in love with the idea of playing Al  Capone that he revived the concept A few  years later Hardy sought the help of  beleaguered director Josh trank who turn  was looking for a comeback after years  of controversy initially titled fonzo  the final result was the streaming  original Capone the late 1990s and early  2000s were Awash with teen horror films  beginning with the wild success of  1996's scream four years later Final  Destination brought the trend into the  New Millennium sparking its own  franchise that saw a slew of sequels  over the next 11 years the first told  the story of an ill-fated Airline flight  whose would-be passengers had been  destined to die but it was almost  something else entirely believe it or  not the premise of Final Destination was  originally developed as a script for the  X-Files the proposed episode titled  flight 180 was fairly similar to what  ended up on the big screen a handful of  passengers exited a doomed flight before  takeoff after one of them has a fateful  premonition only for each of them to die  one by one in other gruesome ways  however in this earlier version the  passenger who had the premonition of the  plane's Ultimate Destruction was  scully's brother lending the story some  serious suspense as the two paranormal  investigators raced against time to save  him according to Legend however writer  Jeffrey Reddick never sent the network  his script having been advised by a  friend at newline Cinema felt the idea  was far too good to be confined to a  single episode of an existing franchise  The Landmark TV series Star Trek dazzled  audiences for three seasons in the 1960s  before low ratings forced its  cancellation but when it got hot in  reruns in the 70s talk of a Revival  surfaced and a feature film seemed like  the next logical step while audiences  were eventually handed a big budget  continuation in 1979 Star Trek the  motion picture started out as a  potential television relaunch under the  title Star Trek phase 2. heading sir  [Music]  developed as a potential Flagship series  for the launch of their own television  network Paramount brought Trek Creator  Gene Roddenberry on board and began  assembling scripts while the studio  resigned the cast to return famously  actor Leonard Nimoy declined apparently  due to tensions with the network over  the sale of merchandise featuring his  likeness nevertheless the remaining cast  was hired the sets were constructed and  the new TV series was all set to go when  a little film from George Lucas landed  in theaters after the success of Star  Wars Paramount pivoted to film they used  much of the work done for Phase 2 even  repurposing its pilot script while also  settling their conflict with Nimoy who  agreed to return his Spock remarkably  many of the original sets for Phase 2  continue to be used on spin-off shows  throughout the 1990s before being torn  down after the conclusion of Star Trek  Voyager the series that had ultimately  launched Paramount's TV network  City of the Dead isn't the kind of movie  you might expect from the title released  in 1960 the movie told the story of a  mysterious town in that turns out to be  controlled by a coven of sinister  witches some men some women whose power  came from the devil gathered beneath the  Ravens Inn to perform a black mass in  the honor of Lucifer initially developed  by screenwriter George baxt as a  television pilot City of the Dead was  intended to be a starry vehicle for  horror icon Force Karloff having found  little success with the networks at the  time producer Milton sabotsky took it  upon himself to fill out the story with  some new characters and City of the Dead  was born Stateside the film was retitled  horror hotel and it remains a cult  favorite of classic horror aficionados  today a teen Thriller film from the  mid-1990s Cruel Intentions is a dark  drama about a pair of Step siblings who  play a twisted game on a celibate school  friend the movie was a modest hit that  grew a fervent fan following so it made  perfect sense that a sequel would follow  the result was Cruel Intentions 2 a  prequel set a few years earlier at a  prestigious boarding school it followed  the exploits of a twisted teen who moves  to a new town and gets involved in some  serious drama of his own Cruel  Intentions 2 was released direct to  video in 2000 it had a noticeably  cheaper look and feel than its  predecessor and a new cast of Mostly  unknowns viewers of the first film who  saw the sequel may have suspected what  we all know now the sequel was  originally filmed as a TV pilot as it  turns out following the success of the  first movie a spin-off prequel titled  Manchester prep was developed and at  least two episodes were filmed  unimpressed with the results and  concerned its envelope pushing story  might not play on network TV the studio  scuttled the project in an attempt to  recoup their investment they chopped up  the episodes and repackaged them as a  directive video feature  foreign  [Music]
 

